WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.690
A teacher posted her class's test

2
00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.550
results in the parents' WhatsApp

3
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:06.710
group, names and all.

4
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.180
She meant well.

5
00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.590
By evening, one child's mark was a

6
00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.970
joke in the whole street.

7
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.580
Today: why pupils' data needs care, and

8
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.240
the simple habits that protect it.

9
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.890
Personal data is anything about a

10
00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.550
person who can be identified: a name,

11
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.930
a photo, a phone number, a mark.

12
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.880
Some of it is sensitive: health,

13
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.520
disability, religion, family troubles.

14
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.030
And children's data needs

15
00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:39.750
the most care of all.

16
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:41.950
Why so careful?

17
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.220
Because data travels, and it stays.

18
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.150
A list shared once can be

19
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.210
forwarded forever.

20
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.030
And a child can't choose what

21
00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.230
adults post about them.

22
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.220
Four habits keep it safe.

23
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.000
Collect only what you need,

24
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.400
for a clear purpose.

25
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.150
Let only the right people see it.

26
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.320
Keep it locked: a phone

27
00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.700
PIN, a cupboard key.

28
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.380
And delete it when it's no longer needed.

29
00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.310
Think of the pupil files in

30
00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:12.990
the office cupboard.

31
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.960
Not everyone gets the key, and the files

32
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.040
don't go home in a teacher's bag.

33
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.020
Treat the data on your phone the same way.

34
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.450
Share the task, not the child.

35
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.970
Post the class average, or

36
00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:29.210
groups by pupil number.

37
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.420
Tell each family their own

38
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.580
child's mark privately.

39
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.540
And ask before you take or share a photo.

40
00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.270
Quick check!

41
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.270
I want the parents' group to

42
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.410
know how the test went.

43
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.540
Which of these can I post there?

44
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.460
The class average: it says how the class

45
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.560
did, without pointing at any child.

46
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.210
Each family then hears their own

47
00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.350
child's mark from you, in private.

48
00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.350
Small facts add up.

49
00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.430
A pupil's school, then her birthday,

50
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.070
then her route home: each harmless

51
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.390
alone, together a map to the child.

52
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.860
In a well-known study, birth date, sex and

53
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.720
postcode alone picked out eighty-seven

54
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.060
people in a hundred in the United States.

55
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.310
The law agrees.

56
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.250
Kenya, Nigeria, Egypt and South Africa all

57
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.050
have data protection laws, and they ask

58
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.130
the same things: a purpose, consent, care,

59
00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.270
and extra care for children.

60
00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.670
In the app, Lina, fourteen, has six

61
00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.330
public posts that each look harmless.

62
00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.750
Read them as a stranger would, then

63
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.210
choose who sees each post.

64
00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:47.260
Can you leave the stranger

65
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.620
knowing nothing useful?

66
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:50.460
Your turn.

67
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.430
A school trip form asks for each

68
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.730
pupil's religion, home address,

69
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:57.990
phone and blood group.

70
00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.470
What should I do?

71
00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.040
Ask only what the trip needs: an emergency

72
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.760
contact and health needs, kept with care.

73
00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.150
Remember: children's data

74
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.410
needs the most care.

75
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.670
Collect only what you need, and lock it.

76
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.740
And share the task, not the child.

77
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.900
Try it in class tomorrow: play “Fine,

78
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.320
care or private” with your pupils.

79
00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.710
Give each group cards: favourite

80
00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:30.150
food, class, home address, phone

81
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.830
number, a photo, a test mark.

82
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.760
They sort them into three piles,

83
00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.220
and say who may see each one.
